<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Find meanings, look up names and define a reusable concept set.
</div>

This chapter uses the public synthetic GiBleed practice connection from [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
**You need:** the packages and the matching practice connection described in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start).
Run the sections in order in one R session. All printed results below were computed
with dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.4. They are teaching data, not clinical findings.

## Search a name

A concept ID identifies a meaning. It is not a patient's ID. Search the GiBleed vocabulary for asthma:

```{r search}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Asthma", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(hits$pooled[c("concept_name", "domain_id", "vocabulary_id")], 8))
```

This is public vocabulary metadata. We show names in tables throughout the book;
numeric IDs occur only in code where they specify what to select.

## Look up known diagnosis concepts

```{r lookup}
answers <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(c(317009L, 80502L), conns = conns)
answers$pooled[c("concept_name", "domain_id")]
```

These are diagnosis meanings. Looking them up does not tell you how many people
had either diagnosis.

## Include descendants deliberately

A *descendant* is a more specific meaning below a parent in a vocabulary hierarchy.
The asthma hierarchy can include more specific diagnosis concepts.

```{r descendants}
desc <- ds.omop.concept.descendants(317009L, include_self = FALSE, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(desc$pooled["concept_name"], 8))
```

Descendants come from `concept_ancestor`. Check their clinical meaning before
including them; a search result is not automatically a study definition.

## Store a set

```{r concept-set}
diagnoses <- omop_concept_set(c(317009L, 80502L))
class(diagnoses)
```

A *concept set* is a reusable selection of concepts. For a focused asthma
definition, keep a separate named set:

```{r question-set}
asthma <- omop_concept_set(317009L)
class(asthma)
```

Both objects are local definitions. Creating one has not selected any patient rows.

## Recap

Use IDs to define selections and names to understand them. Chapter 5 switches to a small synthetic questionnaire fixture, where question and answer concepts have different roles.

## Try it

Look up inpatient and outpatient visit concepts and show only their names.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>


```{r solution}
settings <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(c(9201L, 9202L), conns = conns)
settings$pooled["concept_name"]
```

</details>
